INT. FERRY GALLEY KITCHEN - EVENING

A narrow steel kitchen below decks, swaying gently. Pots hang from magnetic strips. A porthole shows gray swells.

CONSUELO DELGADO, 70s, in an apron and a silver bun, rolls dough on a stainless counter. At the other end, MATEO, 12, peels potatoes into a bucket with great concentration. Beside him sits ISOLDE, 16, in a school sweater, her phone propped against a salt shaker, thumbs idle.

A wall-mounted intercom crackles.

RUDY (V.O.)
(over intercom)
Galley, this is the bridge. Mamá, you got a minute?

Consuelo does not stop rolling. Mateo glances up. Isolde slides her phone face-down.

CONSUELO
(to the intercom)
I have dough.

RUDY (V.O.)
(over intercom)
It's about Isolde's thing.

CONSUELO
Isolde is here. Say it to the room.

A pause. The ferry rocks. Mateo drops a peeled potato into the bucket with a quiet plunk.

RUDY (V.O.)
(over intercom)
It can wait.

CONSUELO
Then it waits.

She flours the pin. Isolde lifts her eyes to the porthole and keeps them there. Consuelo slides a peeled potato across the counter without looking, and Isolde takes it and starts quartering it with a small knife.

MATEO
Abuela, is the bridge mad?

CONSUELO
The bridge is steering.

MATEO
That's not an answer.

CONSUELO
It's a ferry. It's the answer.

Isolde puts the knife down. She picks up the potato quarters, carries them to the pot on the stove, and drops them in one at a time. Consuelo watches her do it. Her shoulders loosen by a fraction of a degree.

CONSUELO (CONT'D)
Salt.

Isolde reaches for the shaker, moving her phone aside. She pinches salt over the pot. Consuelo nods. The intercom clicks once and goes quiet, still listening. Nobody speaks to it.

ISOLDE
(to the pot)
Tell him I'll come up after dinner.

CONSUELO
(to the intercom)
She'll come up after dinner.

The intercom clicks off. Mateo hums. The dough spreads, thinner and thinner.
